## Proof

We determine the cardinality of the set of all chains (totally ordered subsets under $\subseteq$) of the poset $(\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N}), \subseteq)$.

**Upper bound.** Every chain is a subset of $\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})$, so the set of all chains is a subset of $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N}))$. Since $|\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})| = 2^{\aleph_0}$, we have
$$|\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N}))| = 2^{2^{\aleph_0}}.$$
Hence the number of chains is at most $2^{2^{\aleph_0}}$.

**Lower bound.** We construct $2^{2^{\aleph_0}}$ distinct chains. Identify $\mathbb{N}$ with $\mathbb{Q}$ via a bijection (so $\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N}) \cong \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{Q})$). For each real number $r \in \mathbb{R}$, define
$$A_r = \{q \in \mathbb{Q} : q < r\}.$$
If $r < s$, then by density of $\mathbb{Q}$ in $\mathbb{R}$ there exists $q \in \mathbb{Q}$ with $r \leq q < s$, so $q \in A_s \setminus A_r$, giving $A_r \subsetneq A_s$. Thus $\{A_r : r \in \mathbb{R}\}$ is a chain of cardinality $|\mathbb{R}| = 2^{\aleph_0}$, with all elements distinct.

Now for each subset $S \subseteq \mathbb{R}$, define
$$C_S = \{A_r : r \in S\}.$$
Since $C_S$ is a subchain of the chain $\{A_r : r \in \mathbb{R}\}$, it is itself a chain in $(\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{Q}), \subseteq) \cong (\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N}), \subseteq)$.

The map $S \mapsto C_S$ is injective: if $S \neq S'$, say $r \in S \setminus S'$, then $A_r \in C_S$ but $A_r \notin C_{S'}$ (since the $A_r$ are all distinct), so $C_S \neq C_{S'}$.

Therefore the number of chains is at least $|\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R})| = 2^{|\mathbb{R}|} = 2^{2^{\aleph_0}}$.

**Conclusion.** Combining the upper and lower bounds, the cardinality of the set of chains of $(\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N}), \subseteq)$ is exactly
$$\boxed{2^{2^{\aleph_0}}}.$$

